  gcd of |J(C)(F_q)| over the split good primes q < 300 : 240
  |red_q(T)| = 240 at every one of them             : true
  all invariants equal                              : true  ([ 2, 120 ])

  Reduction is injective on the torsion of an abelian variety with good
  reduction at an odd unramified prime, so |J(C)(F)_tors| divides 240; and T is
  a subgroup of J(C)(F)_tors whose reduction already has order 240.  Hence
  ==>  J(C)(F)_tors = T = < t_10, c_spor + fam >  =  [ 2, 120 ]   (order 240)
  (5*t_10 is not in <z> at any of these primes, so |T| >= 2*120 = 240; and D_6,
   of order 3, always lies in <z>, so it is redundant as a generator.)

  In particular J(C)(F)[2] = (Z/2)^2, and the Steiner class eta, a 2-torsion
  class, lies in T: it is NOT an extra generator.

=== Gamma = Z c_spor + J(C)(F)_tors ===
  z = c_spor + fam is torsion, so fam = -c_spor + z lies in Gamma:
  Gamma = < t_10, z, c_spor > = < t_10, D_6, c_spor, fam > contains every known
  Mordell-Weil class (D_6 in <z> and fam = -c_spor + z).
  Write J(C)(F)/tors = Z*ghat, c_spor = beta*ghat, phi_*(ghat) = gamma*P_E.
  phi_* phi^* = 2 gives phi_*(fam) = 2*P_E + tors, and fam = -c_spor + tors,
  so beta*gamma = -2 and therefore  [J(C)(F) : Gamma] = |beta| divides 2.

  local 2-divisibility test (would give index 1 if it ever failed):
    28 split primes tested, 0 of them rule out index 2.
    => the index is not decided locally; the sieve keeps the SAFE multiplier I = 2,
       i.e. j = 2*iota_2 : C^(2)(F) -> Gamma, and takes
          H_q^rob := { y in J(C)(F_q) : 2y in red_q(Gamma) }  as the ambient group,
       which contains red_q(J(C)(F)) whichever of the two values the index has.

